a town in the Upper Palatinate in Bavaria, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumana-thera"><span class="enc-name">Sumana Thera</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumana Thera was a Buddhist monk from Sukhothai who, at the invitation of King Kue Na, came to Chiang Mai in 1369.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumanguru-kante"><span class="enc-name">Sumanguru Kanté</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumanguru Kanté was the ruler of the Sosso Empire in West Africa in the early thirteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumatra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumatra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumatra</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumatra is a large island in western Indonesia, lying along the Strait of Malacca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumatra-treaty"><span class="enc-name">Sumatra Treaty</span><span class="enc-type">Treaty · 1871</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sumatra Treaty was an agreement signed by Britain and the Netherlands in November 1871.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumbawa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumbawa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumbawa is an island in Indonesia's Lesser Sunda chain, between Lombok and Flores.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumbawa-besar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumbawa-besar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumbawa Besar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumbawa Besar is the main town of western Sumbawa in the Indonesian province of West Nusa Tenggara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumedang"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumedang.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumedang</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumedang is a town in the highlands of West Java, Indonesia, north-east of Bandung.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumela-monastery"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumela-monastery.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumela Monastery</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumela Monastery is a Greek Orthodox monastery built into a cliff in the Pontic Mountains near Maçka, south of Trabzon in north-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumer"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumer.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumer</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumer was the region of southern Mesopotamia where one of the world's earliest civilisations developed, with city-states such as Ur, Uruk and Lagash.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-king-list"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumerian-king-list.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumerian King List</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sumerian King List is an ancient Mesopotamian text listing the kings of Sumer and the cities that held kingship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumerian-language"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumerian-language.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumerian language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sumerian language was the language of ancient Sumer, one of the earliest written languages.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumhuram"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumhuram.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumhuram</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumhuram, at Khor Rori on the Dhofar coast of southern Oman, was a fortified port founded by the Kingdom of Hadramawt in the last centuries BCE to control the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumpah"><span class="enc-name">Sumpah</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumpah is a Malay word meaning oath.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumptuary-law"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumptuary-law.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumptuary law</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A sumptuary law is a law that restricts consumption, such as clothing or luxury goods, often according to social rank.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumu-epuh"><span class="enc-name">Sumu-epuh</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumu-epuh was the first known king of Yamhad, ruling from Halab (Aleppo) in northern Syria from about 1810 to 1780 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumu-la-el"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumu-la-el.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumu-la-El</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumu-la-El was an early king of the First Dynasty of Babylon, successor to Sumuabum.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumuabum"><span class="enc-name">Sumuabum</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumuabum was an Amorite chieftain who founded the First Dynasty of Babylon around 1894 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sumy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sumy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumy is a city in north-eastern Ukraine and the centre of Sumy Oblast, on the Psel river close to the Russian border.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sumyafa-ashwa"><span class="enc-name">Sumyafa Ashwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sumyafa Ashwa was a Himyarite Christian installed by the Aksumite king Kaleb as ruler of Himyar in Yemen after the defeat of Yusuf Dhu Nuwas around 525.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-bin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-bin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Bin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Bin was a Chinese military strategist of the Warring States period, said to be a descendant of Sun Wu, author of The Art of War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-ce"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-ce.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Ce</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Ce was a warlord of the late Eastern Han who conquered the lands south of the lower Yangtze in the 190s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-dance"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-dance.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Dance</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sun Dance is a major religious ceremony of many Plains Indigenous peoples, including the Lakota, Cheyenne, Arapaho and Blackfoot.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-en"><span class="enc-name">Sun En</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun En was a leader of the Five Pecks of Rice Daoist sect who led a great rebellion along the south-east coast of Eastern Jin China from 399.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-hao"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-hao.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Hao</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Hao was the last emperor of Eastern Wu, one of the Three Kingdoms of China, reigning from 264 to 280.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-jian"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-jian.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Jian</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Jian was a Chinese general of the late Han dynasty from the lower Yangtze.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-kewang"><span class="enc-name">Sun Kewang</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Kewang was a Chinese military leader and an adopted son of the rebel Zhang Xianzhong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-quan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-quan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Quan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Quan was the founder of the state of Eastern Wu in southern China during the Three Kingdoms period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-tzu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Tzu</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Tzu (Sunzi, Sun Wu) is the traditional author of The Art of War, the most famous Chinese work on military strategy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sun-yat-sen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sun Yat-sen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sun Yat-sen was a Chinese revolutionary and founder of the Kuomintang (Nationalist Party).</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/suna-ii"><span class="enc-name">Suna II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Suna II was kabaka of Buganda from about 1832 to 1856.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-ampel"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Ampel</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Ampel, born Raden Rahmat, was one of the Wali Songo, the nine saints credited with spreading Islam in Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Giri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Giri, born Raden Paku, was one of the Wali Songo, the nine saints credited with spreading Islam in Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Gunung Jati</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Gunung Jati was one of the Wali Songo, the Nine Saints credited with spreading Islam in Java.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunan-prapen"><span class="enc-name">Sunan Prapen</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunan Prapen was the most influential ruler of Giri Kedaton, the religious principality near Gresik in eastern Java founded by Sunan Giri.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunassura"><span class="enc-name">Sunassura</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunassura was a king of Kizzuwatna in south-eastern Anatolia around 1400 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunda-kelapa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sunda-kelapa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sunda Kelapa</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sunda Kelapa was the main port of the Hindu Sunda Kingdom of West Java, at the mouth of the Ciliwung river, exporting pepper.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunda-strait"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sunda-strait.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sunda Strait</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sunda Strait is the channel between Java and Sumatra connecting the Java Sea to the Indian Ocean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sundargarh"><span class="enc-name">Sundargarh</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Sundargarh is a town in northern Odisha, India, and the headquarters of Sundargarh district.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/sunderlal-committee"><span class="enc-name">Sunderlal Committee</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Sunderlal Committee was a goodwill mission sent by the Indian government in late 1948, led by the Congress politician Pandit Sunderlal, to investigate violence in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sundernagar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sundernagar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sundernagar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span cla